@meta
id: 07-05
title: Fall down the file a block at a time
chapter: 07
type: lesson
skills: paragraph-motion
par: 88
minutes: 7
vscode: scroll, scroll, count the blocks with your finger on the screen
open: seed.ts

@brief
Fifteen seed blocks, 104 lines, all the same shape. Three of them are for
non-sterling customers and are missing a currency.

Add a `currency` line directly under `amount` in three of the blocks:

  * the fourth block, `ord_1045`, gets `currency: 'USD',`
  * the ninth block, `ord_1050`, gets `currency: 'EUR',`
  * the thirteenth block, `ord_1054`, gets `currency: 'SEK',`

The other twelve blocks are correct and must be left exactly as they are.

Counting to the thirteenth block with `j` means counting to line 85. There
is a motion that counts blocks instead, and this file is nothing but
blocks.

@keys
| `}` | forward to the next blank line |
| `j` | down a line |
| `o` | open a line below |

@hints
Vim calls a run of text with blank lines around it a paragraph. It does not
care that this is TypeScript — a blank line is a blank line, and your code
is already divided into paragraphs by the blank lines you put between the
blocks.
--
`}` moves forward to the next blank line. Press it three times and you are
at the gap after the third block, which is one `j` from the start of the
fourth.
--
From a blank line, `4j` lands on the `amount` line of the block below it,
and `o` opens the new line under it. Vim keeps the indent for you, so type
`currency: 'USD',` with no leading spaces.

@solution
    } } } 4j o currency: 'USD', <Esc>
    } } } } } 4j o currency: 'EUR', <Esc>
    } } } } 4j o currency: 'SEK', <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Around eighty keystrokes, sixty of them the three lines of text.

The idea is that vim already understands your file as a list of blocks,
because you already write blank lines between things that belong together.
Functions, test cases, config stanzas, log entries, paragraphs of prose,
rows in a fixture file: all of them are paragraphs to vim, and `}` steps
over one whole one per press regardless of how many lines it contains.

That last part is what makes it better than a count of `j`. `5j` means "five
lines", which is a fact about the current formatting and stops being true
the moment someone adds a property. `}` means "the next thing", which stays
true. Motions that describe structure survive edits; motions that describe
distance do not.

Notice also how cheaply the coarse and the fine mix: `}` for the long haul,
`4j` for the last few lines. You do not need one motion that does the whole
journey. You need a short one and a big one, and the habit of using the big
one first.

